    "gp_section_615674426134364979": {
      "settings": {
        "checksum": "8aa2756ab26dca487f92d6c01f537bdba02e890a303c555f83761e19bad80445_1_1_1_0__1",
        "gg7zWXq-7qs_text": "<p><i><strong>Most dog owners try 3-5 different products before finding one that actually works</strong></i></p>",
        "ggPTQ4b2Oyp_text": "<strong>The Biggest Mistake Dog Owners </strong><span style=\"color:#DD1818;\"><strong>Make With Crusty Eyes</strong></span>",
        "ggQd5XkBjkE_srcSet_desktop_src": "https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0735/4833/3323/files/Generated_Image_January_08_2026_-_7_12PM.webp",
        "ggUD4YdXUjF_text": "<p>Those brown tear stains you see every morning? Most owners think they're just cosmetic. <strong>They're not.</strong></p><p>That crusty buildup is bacteria, debris, and allergens accumulating in the warm, moist area around your dog's eyes. And when it sits there day after day, here's what happens:</p><p><strong>Days 1–7:</strong> Bacteria multiplies. Stains darken and become harder to remove.</p><p><strong>Weeks 2–3:</strong> Irritation and redness appear. Your dog starts rubbing their face — introducing more bacteria.</p><p><strong>Week 4+:</strong> What started as cosmetic turns into inflammation. Left untreated, this leads to infections requiring expensive vet visits.</p><p>The problem isn't that you're ignoring it. <strong>Most cleaning methods actually make it worse.</strong></p>"
      },
      "type": "gp-section-615674426134364979"
    },
